@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+/* The model guidance pages. They stand on their own, without the workspace
+   script, so everything here is layout for reading: a table of model facts, a
+   worked sum a reader can redo, and a two-way choice at the top. Colours and
+   spacing come from the variables service.css already defines. */
+.guidance{max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;padding:2.5rem 0 5rem}
+.guidance h2{font-size:clamp(1.4rem,2.6vw,2rem);line-height:1.22;margin:3rem 0 1rem}
+.guidance h3{font-size:1.05rem;margin:2rem 0 .6rem}
+.guidance p,.guidance li{max-width:66ch}
+.guidance ul,.guidance ol{padding-left:1.2rem;margin:1rem 0}
+.guidance li{margin:.5rem 0}
+.model-table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:1.2rem 0;font-size:.92rem}
+.model-table caption{text-align:left;color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;padding-bottom:.6rem}
+.model-table th,.model-table td{border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}
+.model-table thead th{border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);font-size:.85rem;color:var(--muted);font-weight:600}
+.model-table code{font:.88em ui-monospace,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere}
+.sum{border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--paper);border-radius:12px;padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.4rem 0;box-shadow:var(--shadow)}
+.sum h3{margin-top:0}
+.sum ol{counter-reset:none}
+.sum .total{border-top:1px solid var(--border);margin-top:1rem;padding-top:.8rem;font-weight:600}
+.two-ways{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1.5rem;margin:2rem 0}
+.two-ways article{border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--paper);border-radius:12px;padding:1.5rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column}
+.two-ways h2{margin:.4rem 0 .6rem;font-size:1.25rem}
+.two-ways p{color:var(--muted);flex-grow:1}
+.two-ways .button{margin-top:1.2rem;align-self:flex-start}
+.source-note{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;border-left:2px solid var(--border);padding-left:.9rem;margin:1.2rem 0}
+.unmeasured{border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--soft);border-radius:12px;padding:1.4rem}
+.unmeasured h2{margin-top:0}
+.guidance pre{overflow-x:auto}
+.guidance-footer{border-top:1px solid var(--border);margin-top:3.5rem;padding-top:1.5rem;color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem}
+@media(max-width:760px){.two-ways{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr)}
+.model-table{font-size:.85rem}}
diff --git a/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/models-building.html b/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/models-building.html
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..8cd85faa
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/models-building.html
@@ -0,0 +1,147 @@
+<!doctype html>
+<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
+<head>
+  <meta charset="utf-8">
+  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
+  <meta name="description" content="Which model to run for a building step: writing code, drafting a migration, producing a document. Named models local and hosted, the memory arithmetic, and the real setting names.">
+  <title>{{SERVICE_NAME}} | Models for building</title>
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/service.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/model-guidance.css">
+</head>
+<body>
+  <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
+  <header class="header"><a class="brand" href="/"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">↗</span> {{SERVICE_NAME}}</a>
+    <nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a><a href="/models" aria-current="page">Models</a><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a><a href="/connect">Connect</a><a href="/docs">Documentation</a><a href="/app">Workspace</a></nav>
+    <a class="header-access" href="/login">Sign in <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
+  </header>
+  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="guidance" data-guidance-page="building">
+    <p class="eyebrow">Choosing a model · Kind one</p>
+    <h1>Models for building</h1>
+    <p class="lede">A building step writes the thing you keep. The output is long, a mistake shows up in the file, and the bill is mostly the tokens it generated. This page names what to run, shows the memory arithmetic so you can redo it for a model it does not list, and gives the exact settings.</p>
+    <p class="caption">Looking for the other kind of step? <a href="/models/deciding">Classifying and deciding</a> is a different page, because it is different work. <a href="/models">Start at the top</a> if you are not sure which you have.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="what-it-is">What a building step actually is</h2>
+    <p>Three from ordinary coding work:</p>
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>Write the adapter.</strong> A new file with a declared interface, its error handling and its tests. Two hundred lines that either compile or do not.</li>
+      <li><strong>Draft the migration.</strong> Turn a schema change into forward and backward statements, with the data move in the middle. Long, exact and reviewable.</li>
+      <li><strong>Write the guide for the endpoint you just built.</strong> A page of prose with real request and response examples in it.</li>
+    </ul>
+    <p>What these share: one call, a lot of output, and a human or a test looking at the result afterwards. What they do not share with the other kind of work is repetition. You run these a handful of times an afternoon, not four hundred times.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="local">What to run locally</h2>
+    <p>The sizes and context lengths below are what the publishers print on their own pages. They were read on 21 September 2026 and the source is named for each one. Nothing here is a speed or a quality claim.</p>
+
+    <table class="model-table">
+      <caption>Local coding models. Download size and context read from the model's page on the Ollama library, 21 September 2026.</caption>
+      <thead><tr><th>Model tag</th><th>Published size</th><th>Quantisation</th><th>Context the page prints</th><th>What it is for here</th></tr></thead>
+      <tbody>
+        <tr><td><code>qwen2.5-coder:1.5b</code></td><td>986 MB</td><td>default tag</td><td>32K</td><td>Completion and very small edits. Too small to trust with a whole file.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>qwen2.5-coder:7b</code></td><td>4.7 GB</td><td><code>q4_K_M</code></td><td>32K</td><td>The one that fits a 12 GiB card with room for its context. A file at a time.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>qwen2.5-coder:14b</code></td><td>9.0 GB</td><td>default tag</td><td>32K</td><td>A step up if you have 16 GiB or more of video memory and nothing else resident.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>qwen2.5-coder:32b</code></td><td>20 GB</td><td><code>q4_K_M</code></td><td>32K</td><td>Needs a 24 GiB card or larger. It does not fit a 12 GiB one at all.</td></tr>
+      </tbody>
+    </table>
+    <p class="source-note">Sizes, quantisation and context: the Qwen2.5-Coder pages at <code>ollama.com/library/qwen2.5-coder</code> and its tags list, read 21 September 2026. Licence and parameter count: the Hugging Face card at <code>huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct</code>, read 21 September 2026, which prints Apache 2.0, "Number of Parameters: 7.61B", "Number of Paramaters (Non-Embedding): 6.53B", "Number of Layers: 28", and "Full 131,072 tokens" while noting that its <code>config.json</code> is set for context up to 32,768 tokens. The two publishers print different context numbers for the same weights, so read the one your own server reports rather than either page.</p>
+
+    <h3>The one thing measured here</h3>
+    <p>On an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 with 12288 MiB of video memory, running <code>qwen2.5-coder:7b</code> at Q4_K_M through this engine's own local endpoint adapter over a real socket, the model held 6659 MiB and the context length 32768 was read from the server's own model information. The server reported 64 prompt tokens and 18 output tokens. The first call took 87.28 seconds and the next two took 0.71 and 0.78 seconds, because the first one pays to load 4.7 GB of weights.</p>
+    <p>That last line is the one to carry into a schedule. A run that lets the server unload between steps pays the load again at every step. A run that keeps one model resident pays it once. It is a fact about moving weights into video memory, not a measurement of this model's speed or quality, and 18 output tokens is far too few to carry a rate.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="arithmetic">The arithmetic, so you can redo it</h2>
+    <div class="sum" data-arithmetic="fit">
+      <h3>Does this model fit my card?</h3>
+      <ol>
+        <li><strong>Take the published size of the exact tag.</strong> Not the family, the tag. <code>qwen2.5-coder:7b</code> is 4.7 GB; <code>qwen2.5-coder:32b</code> is 20 GB. That is the weights.</li>
+        <li><strong>Add what the server holds on top for the context you allow.</strong> On the machine measured above, the total held was 6659 MiB at context 32768. The published 4.7 GB is 4482 MiB if the publisher's GB means 1,000,000,000 bytes and 4813 MiB if it means 1,073,741,824. So the headroom over the weights was between 1846 and 2177 MiB, for that context, on that server.</li>
+        <li><strong>Compare with your card.</strong> 6659 of 12288 MiB leaves 5629 MiB. That is the slack you have for anything else.</li>
+        <li class="total"><strong>For a model not listed here:</strong> published tag size, plus headroom of that order for a 32K context, against your own video memory. <code>qwen2.5-coder:32b</code> at 20 GB is at least 19073 MiB before any context at all, which is more than the whole 12288 MiB card. It does not fit, and no setting makes it fit.</li>
+      </ol>
+    </div>
+    <p>Only the 6659 MiB figure was measured. Everything after it is arithmetic you should repeat on your own machine, because the headroom depends on your server, your context length and what else is resident.</p>
+    <p>That last point is why the two kinds of work are worth separating on one machine. A small deciding model of about 1.4 GB and this 7B building model can plausibly both stay resident inside 12288 MiB, and then neither pays the load again. A 20 GB building model cannot share the card with anything, so every switch pays the load. Check the sum for your own card before you plan around it.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="hosted">What to run hosted</h2>
+    <p>These are the routes this engine ships. A route is one entry naming a provider, a model and the purposes it may serve; adding a model is adding an entry, not editing code.</p>
+    <table class="model-table">
+      <caption>Routes that declare <code>counted_generation</code>, from <code>default_routes()</code> in <code>model_routes.py</code>.</caption>
+      <thead><tr><th>Route name</th><th>Provider</th><th>Model it names</th><th>Also serves short decisions</th></tr></thead>
+      <tbody>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.default</code></td><td><code>ollama_cloud</code></td><td><code>deepseek-v4-flash:0731</code></td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.hard</code></td><td><code>ollama_cloud</code></td><td><code>deepseek-v4-pro:0813</code></td><td>No</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.glm</code></td><td><code>ollama_cloud</code></td><td><code>glm-5.3-flash</code></td><td>No</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.mistral</code></td><td><code>mistral</code></td><td><code>mistral-small-latest</code></td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.mistral.large</code></td><td><code>mistral</code></td><td><code>mistral-large-latest</code></td><td>No</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.openrouter</code></td><td><code>openrouter</code></td><td><code>deepseek/deepseek-chat</code></td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.openrouter.reasoning</code></td><td><code>openrouter</code></td><td><code>deepseek/deepseek-r1</code></td><td>No</td></tr>
+      </tbody>
+    </table>
+    <p>Each provider carries a declared handshake rather than an assumed one. All three declare that their token counts are provider reported and a context of 131072. <code>mistral</code> and <code>openrouter</code> declare that they support structured output; <code>ollama_cloud</code> declares that it does not, with the provider's own documentation page and the date it was checked written beside the declaration in the source. Those are declarations in this repository's route data, not measurements taken today, so check them against your provider before you rely on one.</p>
+    <p class="source-note">A model name ending in <code>-latest</code> is an alias that moves, so what it points at is whatever the provider says today. For the open weights behind the current Mistral Small line, the Hugging Face card at <code>huggingface.co/mistralai/Mistral-Small-3.2-24B-Instruct-2506</code>, read 21 September 2026, prints the licence as apache-2.0 and the size as 24B params. It does not print a context window; its usage examples set 131072. Do not carry that number over to the hosted alias without checking.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="working">How to tell it is working</h2>
+    <p>For building work the check is usually the thing you were building. The file compiles, the test passes, the migration runs forward and back. That is a stronger check than anything a model can say about itself, and you should prefer it.</p>
+    <p>Three things worth watching that are not the test:</p>
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>The reason the answer ended.</strong> A truncated answer is not a smaller answer, it is a broken one. The measured call above finished for the reason <code>stop</code>; an answer that stopped because it ran out of room needs a larger allowance, not a retry.</li>
+      <li><strong>The output allowance.</strong> Every generation asks for the selected route's source-backed maximum output. A convenient smaller limit is never substituted, and a route whose maximum is unknown stays unavailable until provider discovery or an explicit setting supplies the exact value.</li>
+      <li><strong>What a failed check should do next.</strong> Moving to a larger model is one named response to one named failure, not a general reaction. It is off unless you switch it on.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <h2 id="settings">What the engine does for you, and how to set it</h2>
+    <p>Model routes declare what kind of model they are, how large they are and where they run. Every real call writes a cost record. An unknown cost stays unknown rather than being written as zero. Here is how you configure that for a building step, in <code>loop-engine.settings.yaml</code>.</p>
+
+    <h3>Point a building step at your own server</h3>
+<pre><code>models:
+  default_thinking_power: medium
+  allow_local_counted_generation: true
+  providers:
+    - id: local_coder
+      kind: custom
+      endpoint: http://127.0.0.1:11434
+      model: qwen2.5-coder:7b
+      wire: ollama
+      locality: local
+      credential_env: ""
+      counts_as_evidence: false
+      purposes: [counted_generation]
+  tiers:
+    small:
+      routes: [custom.local_coder, cloud.mistral, cloud.default]
+      timeout_seconds: 120
+      max_attempts: 2</code></pre>
+    <p>Field by field, and every one of these exists:</p>
+    <ul>
+      <li><code>id</code> becomes the route name. A provider with <code>id: local_coder</code> is reachable as the route <code>custom.local_coder</code>, which is the name you write in a tier.</li>
+      <li><code>wire</code> is <code>openai</code> or <code>ollama</code>. Those are the two wire formats; almost every self-hosted server speaks one of them.</li>
+      <li><code>credential_env</code> names an environment variable, never a key. Leave it empty for a keyless local server. No key is ever written into this file.</li>
+      <li><code>purposes</code> is what the route is allowed to serve. <code>counted_generation</code> is the building purpose. The full set is <code>counted_generation</code>, <code>decide_label</code>, <code>generation</code>, <code>reasoning</code>, <code>code</code>, <code>vision</code>, <code>tool_use</code>, <code>embedding</code>, <code>reranking</code>, <code>query_rewrite</code> and <code>structured_extract</code>.</li>
+      <li><code>allow_local_counted_generation</code> is the switch that lets a local route do building work at all. It is <code>false</code> by default, and with it false a local route asking for <code>counted_generation</code> is refused with a message that names this exact setting rather than quietly falling back.</li>
+      <li><code>counts_as_evidence</code> is separate and is <code>false</code> by default. A self-hosted server can report honest token counts; whether a comparison may cite them is a judgement about whether another machine would reproduce them, so it has to be declared rather than assumed.</li>
+      <li><code>tiers</code> maps a thinking level to an ordered list of routes with a timeout and an attempt limit. The levels are <code>small</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>high</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>specialized</code>. The shipped lists are described in the source as conservative route hints and not a measured quality ranking, which is exactly what they are.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <h3>If you want a failed output check to try a larger model</h3>
+<pre><code>models:
+  escalation:
+    enabled: true
+    order: [small, medium, high, max]
+    on_errors: [output_validation_failed]
+    max_tier_changes: 1</code></pre>
+    <p><code>enabled</code> is <code>false</code> by default. <code>on_errors</code> names which failures may move up, so a provider outage is not treated as the model being too small. <code>max_tier_changes</code> bounds how far one step may climb. <code>specialized</code> cannot appear in <code>order</code>, because it is chosen by what the task needs and not by escalating power.</p>
+
+    <h3>Reading what it cost</h3>
+    <p>Each implementation of each step writes one cost record carrying its <code>operation_id</code>, its <code>implementation_id</code>, its <code>run_id</code>, an <code>outcome</code> of <code>verified</code>, <code>failed</code> or <code>unknown</code>, and timings for the <code>setup</code>, <code>execution</code>, <code>verification</code> and <code>recovery</code> phases, together with <code>model_calls</code>, <code>input_tokens</code>, <code>output_tokens</code> and <code>monetary_cost</code>.</p>
+    <p>A record counts as complete only when every one of those four phases has a known timing, and only complete records are averaged. A token count that is missing or malformed is kept as unknown; it never becomes zero. This matters most for exactly the comparison you would want to make here, because a local route that reports nothing would otherwise look free.</p>
+
+    <div class="unmeasured" data-unmeasured="building">
+      <h2>What nobody has measured</h2>
+      <p>No saving is claimed on this page, and none has been measured. What exists is one record of one local model answering three times on one machine, and that record deliberately carries no rate, no quality judgement and no comparison. Nobody here has run the same building task through a local model and a hosted one and compared the results, the cost or the time. Nobody has graded the output of any model named above. The tier lists this engine ships were written as sensible starting points by people reasoning about model sizes, not derived from a measurement, and the source says so. Treat every recommendation here as a starting configuration to test on your own work, and keep your own cost records, because those will be about your tasks rather than somebody else's.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <footer class="guidance-footer">
+      <p><a href="/models">Which model for this step</a> · <a href="/models/deciding">Classifying and deciding</a> · <a href="/docs">Setup guide</a> · <a href="/">Home</a></p>
+    </footer>
+  </main>
+</body>
+</html>
diff --git a/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/models-deciding.html b/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/models-deciding.html
