index 00000000..5d92c6f6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/loop_engine/core/service_runtime/web_assets/models-deciding.html
@@ -0,0 +1,163 @@
+<!doctype html>
+<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
+<head>
+  <meta charset="utf-8">
+  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
+  <meta name="description" content="Which model to run for a classifying or deciding step: is this file relevant, does this answer satisfy the contract, which approach fits. Named models, the answer shape, and how to measure agreement.">
+  <title>{{SERVICE_NAME}} | Models for classifying and deciding</title>
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/service.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/model-guidance.css">
+</head>
+<body>
+  <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
+  <header class="header"><a class="brand" href="/"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">↗</span> {{SERVICE_NAME}}</a>
+    <nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a><a href="/models" aria-current="page">Models</a><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a><a href="/connect">Connect</a><a href="/docs">Documentation</a><a href="/app">Workspace</a></nav>
+    <a class="header-access" href="/login">Sign in <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
+  </header>
+  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="guidance" data-guidance-page="deciding">
+    <p class="eyebrow">Choosing a model · Kind two</p>
+    <h1>Models for classifying and deciding</h1>
+    <p class="lede">A deciding step returns a label, a choice between named options, or a small record. The output is short, the step runs many times, and the bill is the number of calls and the input each one carries. This is the work that most often runs on a model far larger than it needs, because nobody ever said what else to run.</p>
+    <p class="caption">Writing code, a migration or a document instead? That is <a href="/models/building">the other page</a>. <a href="/models">Start at the top</a> if you are not sure which you have.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="what-it-is">What a deciding step actually is</h2>
+    <p>Three from ordinary coding work:</p>
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>Is this file relevant to the change I am making?</strong> Forty candidates come back from a search. Forty calls, each one returning yes or no, each one carrying a file path and a few hundred tokens of the file.</li>
+      <li><strong>Does this answer satisfy the contract the step declared?</strong> One call per candidate answer, returning accept, repair or reject. The answer you are judging is the input; the output is one word.</li>
+      <li><strong>Is this failure the same as one we have already seen?</strong> A new stack trace against the ones already recorded. The output is a name or nothing.</li>
+    </ul>
+    <p>A fourth that is worth naming separately, because it looks like reasoning and is not: <strong>which of these five approaches fits this task?</strong> Five named options, a short reason, and a move to the next step. That is a choice between declared candidates, not an essay.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="arithmetic">Where the money goes, and how to check</h2>
+    <div class="sum" data-arithmetic="calls">
+      <h3>Put your own numbers in. These are placeholders, not a measurement.</h3>
+      <ol>
+        <li>Say a relevance step runs over 40 candidates, 10 times in an afternoon. That is 400 calls.</li>
+        <li>Say each call carries 2,000 tokens in: the question, the candidate names and the piece of file you are judging.</li>
+        <li>Input: 400 × 2,000 = 800,000 tokens.</li>
+        <li>Output: 400 × about 20 tokens for a label, a probability and a short reason = 8,000 tokens.</li>
+        <li class="total">Input is 100 times the output. Every price list charges for both, so for this work you are buying input, and the lever that matters is how much you carry into each call, not which model writes the nicest sentence.</li>
+      </ol>
+    </div>
+    <p>Two consequences follow. Send less into each call, which is what selecting material before loading it is for. And run the call somewhere cheap, which is what the rest of this page is about.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="local">What to run locally</h2>
+    <p>Sizes and context lengths below are what the publishers print on their own pages, read on 21 September 2026, with the source named. Nothing here is a speed or a quality claim.</p>
+    <table class="model-table">
+      <caption>Small local models for label and choice work. Size and context read from the Ollama library pages, 21 September 2026.</caption>
+      <thead><tr><th>Model tag</th><th>Published size</th><th>Context the page prints</th><th>What it is for here</th></tr></thead>
+      <tbody>
+        <tr><td><code>qwen3:0.6b</code></td><td>523 MB</td><td>40K</td><td>The narrowest labels, where the candidates are few and obvious. Expect it to decline more often.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>qwen3:1.7b</code></td><td>1.4 GB</td><td>40K</td><td>The first one to try. Small enough to sit beside a building model on one 12 GiB card.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>qwen3:8b</code></td><td>5.2 GB</td><td>40K</td><td>When the 1.7B declines too often on your own suite, and only then.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>qwen2.5-coder:1.5b</code></td><td>986 MB</td><td>32K</td><td>Labels about code specifically, where the input is source rather than prose.</td></tr>
+      </tbody>
+    </table>
+    <p class="source-note">Sizes and context: the Qwen3 and Qwen2.5-Coder pages and tag lists at <code>ollama.com/library/qwen3</code> and <code>ollama.com/library/qwen2.5-coder</code>, read 21 September 2026, where the <code>qwen3:1.7b</code> tag is listed both plain and as <code>qwen3:1.7b-q4_K_M</code> at the same 1.4 GB. Licence and parameter count: the Hugging Face card at <code>huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-1.7B</code>, read 21 September 2026, which prints apache-2.0, 1.7B parameters with 1.4B non-embedding, 28 layers, and a 32,768 token context. The two publishers print different context numbers for the same weights, so read the one your own server reports rather than either page.</p>
+
+    <h3>Fitting a deciding model beside a building model</h3>
+    <p>The one figure measured here: on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 with 12288 MiB of video memory, <code>qwen2.5-coder:7b</code> at Q4_K_M held 6659 MiB with context length 32768, read from the server's own model information. Its first call took 87.28 seconds against 0.71 and 0.78 seconds for the two after it, because the first one pays to load 4.7 GB of weights.</p>
+    <p>So the question for a second model is whether it fits in what is left. 12288 minus 6659 leaves 5629 MiB. A 1.4 GB model is 1335 MiB if the publisher's GB means 1,000,000,000 bytes and 1434 MiB if it means 1,073,741,824, before whatever the server holds on top for its context. That leaves real room, and two resident models never pay the load again. Only the 6659 MiB is measured. Read your own number the same way, from your server or from your card's own reporting, before you plan a night around it.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="hosted">What to run hosted</h2>
+    <p>Three of the routes this engine ships declare that they may serve short decisions as well as building work. A route is an entry naming a provider, a model and the purposes it is allowed to serve.</p>
+    <table class="model-table">
+      <caption>Routes that declare <code>decide_label</code>, from <code>default_routes()</code> in <code>model_routes.py</code>.</caption>
+      <thead><tr><th>Route name</th><th>Provider</th><th>Model it names</th><th>Provider declares structured output</th></tr></thead>
+      <tbody>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.mistral</code></td><td><code>mistral</code></td><td><code>mistral-small-latest</code></td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.openrouter</code></td><td><code>openrouter</code></td><td><code>deepseek/deepseek-chat</code></td><td>Yes</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>cloud.default</code></td><td><code>ollama_cloud</code></td><td><code>deepseek-v4-flash:0731</code></td><td>No</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>local.slm</code></td><td><code>ollama_local</code></td><td>a local small model, wired and permitted for decisions</td><td>Not declared</td></tr>
+      </tbody>
+    </table>
+    <p>The structured output column is a declaration carried in this repository's route data, with the provider documentation page and the date it was checked written beside it in the source. It matters here because a provider that constrains decoding to a schema returns the answer shape more reliably than one that is merely asked for JSON in a prompt. It does not replace the check below: prompted JSON is not provider-enforced JSON Schema, and the answer is admitted or refused on its own merits either way.</p>
+    <p class="source-note">For the open weights behind the current Mistral Small line, the Hugging Face card at <code>huggingface.co/mistralai/Mistral-Small-3.2-24B-Instruct-2506</code>, read 21 September 2026, prints the licence as apache-2.0 and the size as 24B params. A name ending in <code>-latest</code> is an alias that moves, so check what your provider points it at today.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="working">How to tell it is working</h2>
+    <p>This is the part that matters. A building model that is wrong leaves a bug you can read. A deciding model that is wrong returns a confident label, nothing looks broken, and the wrong file goes into the next step. <strong>A decision model that always answers confidently is worse than one that declines</strong>, because you cannot tell its good answers from its bad ones.</p>
+
+    <h3>1. A declared answer shape</h3>
+    <p>A bounded decision goes out under the response contract named <code>typed_decision.choice</code>. The request carries the question, the declared candidates and the evidence, and nothing else is weighed. At most ten candidates: a wider choice is refused with a message telling you to split it, rather than quietly weighing twenty.</p>
+    <p>The answer is one JSON object with these fields:</p>
+    <table class="model-table">
+      <caption>The typed decision response contract.</caption>
+      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead>
+      <tbody>
+        <tr><td><code>chosen</code></td><td>The candidate taken. Empty when the answer is a refusal to decide.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>rows</code></td><td>One row for each declared candidate, each carrying <code>candidate</code> and <code>confidence</code>.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>confidence</code></td><td>How confident the judge is overall, between zero and one.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>abstain</code></td><td>A Boolean. True when the judge will not choose.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>reason</code></td><td>Short text. Optional.</td></tr>
+      </tbody>
+    </table>
+
+    <h3>2. A refusal when the model cannot answer, and a refusal when it answers wrongly</h3>
+    <p>Admission refuses an answer by name rather than repairing it. It refuses when the answer is not a JSON object, when any of <code>chosen</code>, <code>rows</code>, <code>confidence</code> or <code>abstain</code> is missing, when <code>rows</code> is empty, when a row lacks <code>candidate</code> or <code>confidence</code>, when the same candidate appears in two rows, when the rows do not name exactly the declared candidates, when the rows sum more than 0.01 away from one, when <code>abstain</code> is not a Boolean, when <code>chosen</code> is not one of the declared candidates, and when <code>chosen</code> is not the row carrying the highest confidence. The refusal prints what was actually there, including the extra and missing candidates and the sum the rows really came to.</p>
+    <p>That last rule is the one people are surprised by. A model that writes a confident paragraph choosing option B while its own probability rows put the mass on option C is not making a decision, it is making two, and the answer is refused rather than averaged.</p>
+    <p>Declining is a first-class answer, not an error. An answer with <code>abstain</code> true and no <code>chosen</code> is admitted, recorded as an abstention, and passed back for the step to handle. The small judge that ships with this engine abstains when it recognises none of the declared candidates, instead of spreading its mass over names it has never seen.</p>
+
+    <h3>3. A way to measure agreement against known-right cases</h3>
+    <p>Write a suite. A suite has an identifier, a version and a list of cases. Each case has its own identifier, the request, and <code>expected</code>: the candidate that is right, or empty when the right answer is to decline. Run it against a route and a judge and read the report:</p>
+    <table class="model-table">
+      <caption>What one suite report carries, per judge and per route.</caption>
+      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
+      <tbody>
+        <tr><td><code>total</code>, <code>decided</code>, <code>abstained</code>, <code>errored</code></td><td>Exact denominators. The report refuses to exist unless <code>total</code> equals the other three added together, so nothing goes missing.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>correct</code>, <code>wrong</code></td><td>Of the ones it decided. <code>decided</code> must equal these two added together.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>accuracy_over_decided</code></td><td><code>correct</code> divided by <code>decided</code>. Over what it decided, not over the whole suite, so declining cannot inflate it.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>errors</code></td><td>The case identifier and the error type for each case that threw. An error is counted as an error and never as a pass.</td></tr>
+        <tr><td><code>mean_confidence_correct</code>, <code>mean_confidence_wrong</code></td><td>The two numbers that catch a confident guesser.</td></tr>
+      </tbody>
+    </table>
+    <p><strong>Read the last two together.</strong> If a judge is right at 0.9 and wrong at 0.5, its confidence carries information and you can set a threshold below which the case goes to a bigger model or to a person. If it is right at 0.9 and wrong at 0.9, its confidence tells you nothing, there is no threshold to set, and a higher accuracy number is worth less than it looks.</p>
+    <p><strong>Put known-wrong cases in the suite.</strong> Cases whose right answer is to decline, with <code>expected</code> left empty, are the control. A judge that never declines fails them, and the report shows it in <code>abstained</code> and <code>wrong</code> rather than in a number that still looks respectable. Cases naming a candidate the judge has never seen belong there too.</p>
+    <p>Run the same suite against every route you are considering. The report names the route, so two runs are directly comparable, and comparing a 1.7B model on your own machine against a hosted route on your own cases is the only comparison that answers your question.</p>
+
+    <h2 id="settings">What the engine does for you, and how to set it</h2>
+    <p>Model routes declare what kind of model they are, how large they are and where they run. Every real call writes a cost record. An unknown cost stays unknown rather than being written as zero. Here is how you configure a deciding route, in <code>loop-engine.settings.yaml</code>.</p>
+<pre><code>models:
+  providers:
+    - id: local_decider
+      kind: custom
+      endpoint: http://127.0.0.1:11434
+      model: qwen3:1.7b
+      wire: ollama
+      locality: local
+      credential_env: ""
+      counts_as_evidence: false
+      purposes: [decide_label]
+      think: "off"
+  tiers:
+    small:
+      routes: [custom.local_decider, cloud.mistral, cloud.default]
+      timeout_seconds: 120
+      max_attempts: 2</code></pre>
+    <p>Field by field, and every one of these exists:</p>
+    <ul>
+      <li><code>purposes: [decide_label]</code> is what makes this a deciding route. It is the narrow purpose, and it is <strong>permitted for a local route by default</strong>. Unlike building work, you do not switch anything on: a local model may decide and label, it may not be the large generation workhorse.</li>
+      <li><code>id</code> becomes the route name. <code>id: local_decider</code> is reachable as the route <code>custom.local_decider</code>, which is the name you write in a tier.</li>
+      <li><code>wire</code> is <code>openai</code> or <code>ollama</code>. Those are the two wire formats.</li>
+      <li><code>credential_env</code> names an environment variable, never a key. Leave it empty for a keyless local server.</li>
+      <li><code>think</code> matters here more than anywhere. On the Ollama wire the values are <code>default</code>, <code>off</code>, <code>on</code> and <code>model</code>. <code>off</code> sends <code>think: false</code> so the whole output allowance goes to the answer. The reason is written into the source: a reasoning model spends its output ceiling thinking before it emits anything, and the structured answer comes back truncated. For a step whose entire output is a label and a few probability rows, that is the failure you will hit first.</li>
+      <li><code>counts_as_evidence</code> is <code>false</code> by default. A self-hosted server can report honest token counts; whether a comparison may cite them is a judgement about whether another machine would reproduce them, so it is declared rather than assumed.</li>
+      <li><code>tiers</code> maps a thinking level to an ordered list of routes. Putting your local route first in <code>small</code> and leaving hosted routes behind it is what sends the many cheap calls to your own machine while keeping a way through when it is not running.</li>
+    </ul>
+    <p>Two neighbouring purposes behave differently and it is worth knowing before you hit them. <code>query_rewrite</code> and <code>structured_extract</code> are also bounded local jobs, and both are refused for a local route by default with a message saying they are disabled by policy. They are switches in the route policy rather than fields in this settings file today, so a local route declaring either of them will be refused until that changes.</p>
+
+    <h3>Reading what it cost</h3>
+    <p>Each implementation of each step writes one cost record carrying its <code>operation_id</code>, its <code>implementation_id</code>, its <code>run_id</code>, an <code>outcome</code> of <code>verified</code>, <code>failed</code> or <code>unknown</code>, timings for the <code>setup</code>, <code>execution</code>, <code>verification</code> and <code>recovery</code> phases, and <code>model_calls</code>, <code>input_tokens</code>, <code>output_tokens</code> and <code>monetary_cost</code>. A record is complete only when all four phase timings are known, and only complete records are averaged. A token count that is missing or malformed stays unknown and never becomes zero.</p>
+    <p>For this kind of work that rule is doing real protection. A local route that reports no counts would otherwise appear to cost nothing, and the step that runs four hundred times is exactly where a free-looking number would do the most damage to a comparison.</p>
+
+    <div class="unmeasured" data-unmeasured="deciding">
+      <h2>What nobody has measured</h2>
+      <p>No saving is claimed on this page, and none has been measured. Nobody here has run a suite of real deciding cases against a small local model and a hosted one and compared the agreement, the cost or the time. There is no measured accuracy figure for any model named above, on any task. The one local measurement that exists is three calls of one coding model on one machine, and it deliberately carries no rate and no quality judgement. The example call counts in the arithmetic above are placeholders for your own numbers, not observations. Even the shape of the advice, that small models are enough for narrow labelled choices, is a reasonable expectation rather than a result: it is why the suite and its two confidence numbers exist, so that you can find out on your own cases instead of taking it from a page.</p>
+    </div>
+
