+<!doctype html>
+<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
+<head>
+  <meta charset="utf-8">
+  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
+  <meta name="description" content="Which model should you run for this step? Building work and deciding work need different models. Start here and take the page for the kind of step you have.">
+  <title>{{SERVICE_NAME}} | Which model for this step</title>
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/service.css">
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/model-guidance.css">
+</head>
+<body>
+  <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
+  <header class="header"><a class="brand" href="/"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">↗</span> {{SERVICE_NAME}}</a>
+    <nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a><a href="/models" aria-current="page">Models</a><a href="/pricing">Pricing</a><a href="/connect">Connect</a><a href="/docs">Documentation</a><a href="/app">Workspace</a></nav>
+    <a class="header-access" href="/login">Sign in <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
+  </header>
+  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="guidance" data-guidance-page="index">
+    <p class="eyebrow">Choosing a model</p>
+    <h1>Which model should this step run?</h1>
+    <p class="lede">A task is not one job. Writing a migration and deciding whether a file is relevant are different work, they fail in different ways, and the money goes to different places. Running the same large model for both is the expensive habit this page is here to break.</p>
+
+    <p>Answer one question about the step in front of you.</p>
+
+    <p><strong>Is the model's output the thing you keep, or is it a decision about something else you keep?</strong></p>
+
+    <div class="two-ways">
+      <article data-work-kind="building">
+        <p class="eyebrow">Kind one</p>
+        <h2>Building</h2>
+        <p>You keep what the model writes. Code, a migration, a document, a test. The output is long, a mistake is visible in the file, and what you pay for is mostly the tokens it generates.</p>
+        <a class="button primary" href="/models/building">Models for building <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
+      </article>
+      <article data-work-kind="deciding">
+        <p class="eyebrow">Kind two</p>
+        <h2>Classifying and deciding</h2>
+        <p>You keep something else. The model returns a label, a choice between named options, or a small record. The output is short, the step runs many times, and what you pay for is the number of calls and the input each one carries.</p>
+        <a class="button primary" href="/models/deciding">Models for deciding <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
+      </article>
+    </div>
+
+    <h2>Why the split is worth making</h2>
+    <p>A building step might run four times in an afternoon and produce two hundred lines each time. A deciding step might run four hundred times in the same afternoon and produce one word each time. Those two shapes want different models, different memory, and different checks that they are working.</p>
+    <p>They also fail differently. A building model that is slightly wrong leaves a bug you can read. A deciding model that is slightly wrong returns a confident label, nothing looks broken, and the wrong file goes into the next step. That is why the deciding page spends most of its length on how to tell it is working.</p>
+
+    <h2>What holds for both</h2>
+    <ul>
+      <li><strong>A route is a setting, not a branch in the code.</strong> Each way of reaching a model is one entry with a provider, a model name, where it runs, and the purposes it is allowed to serve. Adding a model is adding an entry.</li>
+      <li><strong>Every real call writes a cost record.</strong> One record for each implementation of each step, with its phase timings, its call count, its token counts and its outcome.</li>
+      <li><strong>An unknown cost stays unknown.</strong> A missing token count is recorded as unknown, never as zero, and a record with a missing phase is never averaged as if it were complete. A comparison built on unknowns says so instead of flattering the cheap option.</li>
+    </ul>
+    <p>Both pages end with the same honest paragraph: what nobody here has measured. Neither page claims a saving.</p>
+
+    <h2>Before you change anything</h2>
+    <p>Your own tools make your model calls with your own provider account. Nothing on these pages asks for a model key, and this service never receives one. Read <a href="/security">what you share and what can run</a> if that boundary matters to you, and <a href="/connect">connect your client</a> when you are ready.</p>
+
+    <footer class="guidance-footer">
+      <p><a href="/models/building">Building</a> · <a href="/models/deciding">Classifying and deciding</a> · <a href="/docs">Setup guide</a> · <a href="/">Home</a></p>
+    </footer>
+  </main>
+</body>
+</html>
